<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke Wen of Jin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">697–628 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Chong'er</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duke Wen of Jin, born Chong'er, spent nineteen years in exile among other states before taking the throne of Jin in 636 BCE. He invaded Cao in 632 BCE, defeated Chu at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chengpu">Battle of Chengpu</a> the same year and was recognised as hegemon, one of the Five Hegemons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-jin.jpg" alt="Duke Wen of Jin" width="175" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Li Tang · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duke_Wen_of_Jin_(%E6%99%89%E6%96%87%E5%85%AC).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chong'er, of the Ji clan, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-xian-of-jin">Duke Xian of Jin</a>; Wen, the Cultured, is his posthumous name. In his father's later years the concubine Li Ji schemed to make her son Xiqi heir, and the crown prince Shensheng took his own life in 656 BCE. In the civil war that followed, the Li Ji Unrest, Duke Xian campaigned against his own sons. Chong'er fled north with able followers, among them Zhao Cui, Hu Yan, Xian Zhen and Jie Zhitui. In 651 BCE he refused an offer of the throne, which went to his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-hui-of-jin">Yiwu</a>, Duke Hui of Jin.</p><p>After Duke Hui's attempts to have him killed he moved in 644 BCE to Qi, and from 639 BCE he wandered through Cao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Zheng, Chu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. After Duke Hui's death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-mu-of-qin">Duke Mu of Qin</a> escorted him home with an army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Duke Wen reorganised the state into three armies, upper, middle and lower, each under a general and a lieutenant-general, and gave the men of his exile high posts. Jin absorbed many neighbouring states and made others <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. He backed the Eastern Zhou court, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-xiang-of-zhou">King Xiang of Zhou</a> was driven out by his brother in 635 BCE, Duke Wen's coalition restored him.</p><p>When Chu invaded Jin's ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> in 633 BCE, he led troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, Qi and Song against it. Legend says he kept a promise made at the Chu court by pulling back three she, about 30 km, before the battle, and used the retreat to draw the Chu commander Ziyu into an ambush. At a meeting of rulers at Jiantu he was confirmed as hegemon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in 628 BCE and was succeeded by his son Duke Xiang of Jin, and the hegemony stayed with Jin for nearly a century. His reforms kept Jin stable and strong for decades, but the houses of Wei and Zhao, raised by their military posts at his court, later came to dominate it, leading to the partition of Jin two and a half centuries later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> tells how his follower Jie Zhitui refused reward and withdrew to Mount Mian; later legend has Jie die in a forest fire set to drive him out, an origin story of the Cold Food Festival, which is not attested before the Han.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duke Wen of Jin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its most famous episode came when Duke Gong of Cao spied on the exiled <b>Prince Chong'er</b> of Jin in his bath to see his reputedly joined ribs.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-cao">State of Cao</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Xian expanded Jin by conquering neighbours such as Western Guo and the State of Yu, and <b>Duke Wen</b>, the long-exiled Chong'er, became hegemon after defeating the State of Chu at Chengpu in 632 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-jin">State of Jin</a></li></ul></section>
